WEBVTT

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:22.970
<v Ira Glass>OK, you know the rules. No smoking in here. Cell phones off. No talking during the show. Ready? OK, let's roll the film. This is a home movie, and it looks like any other home movie. People at a summer resort. White cottages with screened-in porches and red roofs. There's a woman with an 18-month-old girl running around.
<v Ira Glass>好的，你知道规矩。这里禁止吸烟。手机关机。放映期间禁止说话。准备好了吗？好的，电影开演吧。这是一部家庭录像，它看起来和别的家庭录像没什么两样。人们在避暑胜地。有着带纱窗门廊和红色屋顶的白色小屋。有个女人带着一个 18 个月大的女孩在四处跑。

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:30.230
<v Kathy Alexander>There I am. Aren't I cute? Look at that. Chubby. Some things just don't change.
<v Kathy Alexander>那是我。我不可爱吗？看那个。胖乎乎的。有些事情就是不会改变。

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:51.420
<v Ira Glass>Kathy Alexander is the 18-month-old in the film, now all grown up. What interests her in this film is the woman. Kathy is adopted, and she thinks that this woman, whose name she does not know, might be her real mother. The woman's face is mostly obscured because they're in the shade. It's fuzzy. The film's old, and Kathy strains to see the resemblance.
<v Ira Glass>凯西·亚历山大就是影片里那个 18 个月大的女孩，现在已经长大了。她对这部影片感兴趣的是那个女人。凯西是被领养的，她认为这个连名字都不知道的女人，可能是她的亲生母亲。那个女人的脸大部分被遮住了，因为她们在阴影里。画面很模糊。影片很老了，凯西努力想从中看出相似之处。

00:00:51.420 --> 00:01:36.940
<v Kathy Alexander>Oh, here she is. See, almost right in here, you could see her face. And if I could just get that lightened-- some point right here-- see, what happens is, it's lighter, and again, there's my profile. And then right here, this right here, is my posture. Just looking at that looks like I'm looking at myself on one of my other home videos. And here-- if you could take a look, if I can even just show you that thigh. I mean, nobody's got thighs like that. Those are mine. That's a hereditary feature because I could see my daughter has, just like I did.
<v Kathy Alexander>噢，她在这儿。你看，就在这儿，几乎能看到她的脸。如果我能把光调亮一点——就在这儿——看，发生什么了，这里更亮一点，还有，那是我的侧脸。然后就在这儿，这一点，是我的姿势。光是看着这个，就像在看我其他家庭录像里的自己。还有这儿——如果你能看看，如果我能给你看那个大腿。我是说，没人有那样的大腿。那是我的。那是遗传特征，因为我能看到我女儿也有，就像我小时候一样。

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:58.140
<v Ira Glass>She watches this minute of film over and over. She gives copies of it to friends the way that other people hand out pictures of their kids. It's one of the most important things she owns. But to look at it, you'd never know because all home movies look alike. When they mean anything, it's small and it's private. The pictures themselves don't tell much. You know what I'm talking about.
<v Ira Glass>她一遍又一遍地看这一分钟的录像。她像别人分发孩子照片那样把拷贝送给朋友。这是她拥有的最重要的东西之一。但你看的时候，你永远不会知道，因为所有家庭录像看起来都一样。当它们有意义时，那也是微小且私人的。画面本身讲不出太多东西。你知道我在说什么。

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:05.440
<v Alan Berliner>Birthday parties, weddings, rites of passage. First steps. Babies in bassinets.
<v Alan Berliner>生日派对，婚礼，成长仪式。蹒跚学步。摇篮里的婴儿。

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:15.330
<v Ira Glass>Alan Berliner spent six years of his life going around finding old home movies and watching them and collecting them and finally condensing them into this amazing film that he called Family Album.
<v Ira Glass>艾伦·伯林纳花了他生命中六年的时间四处寻找旧家庭录像，观看、收集，最终将它们浓缩成这部他称之为《家庭相册》的神奇影片。

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.090
<v Alan Berliner>There's invariably the baby that they put on the back of the family dog.
<v Alan Berliner>总会有那个被他们放在家庭宠物狗背上的婴儿。

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.440
<v Ira Glass>That's a standard shot? You've seen that a lot?
<v Ira Glass>那是标准镜头吗？你见过很多次？

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.220
<v Alan Berliner>I've seen several of that. Yes. The beach is also big, big.
<v Alan Berliner>我见过好几次。是的。海滩也是重头戏，非常重。

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:26.540
<v Ira Glass>Big.
<v Ira Glass>非常。

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.500
<v Alan Berliner>Big time beach. Summer beach.
<v Alan Berliner>海滩重头戏。夏季海滩。

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.770
<v Ira Glass>The beach figures bigger into our home movies than into our lives.
<v Ira Glass>海滩在我们家庭录像中的戏份比在我们生活中还要重。

00:02:33.770 --> 00:03:12.180
<v Alan Berliner>Yeah, I would say. Swimming is a big thing that Earthlings do, according to home movies. Life on earth is a life of leisure without struggle that, again, mostly takes place in the summer, in the water, at beaches, or around pools. Home movies are also primarily about children. Perhaps 60%, 70% percent of home movies contain images of children, I would say up to about the age of 12 or so. And then it stops.
<v Alan Berliner>是啊，我会这么说。根据家庭录像，游泳是地球人做的一件大事。地球上的生活是一种没有挣扎的闲暇生活，再次强调，它大多发生在夏天，在水里，在海滩上，或者在泳池边。家庭录像也主要是关于孩子的。我会说，60% 到 70% 的家庭录像包含孩子们的影像，大概到 12 岁左右。然后就停止了。

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:40.230
<v Alan Berliner>In fact, I've seen many, many collections of home movies, and they'll be-- for argument's sake, imagine a girl named Adrian. The cans will be labeled, "Adrian, Age 1," "Adrian, Age 2," "Age 3," "Age 4." And at about age 11 or 12, it stops. There's no more Adrian until, perhaps, she gets married. And that's because puberty happens, adolescence happens, and you suddenly have a kind of tension within the family in terms of the relation between parent and child.
<v Alan Berliner>事实上，我见过许多许多家庭录像合集，它们会是——比方说，想象一个叫艾德丽安的女孩。带子会被贴上标签，“艾德丽安，1岁”，“艾德丽安，2岁”，“3岁”，“4岁”。在大约 11 或 12 岁时，就停了。再也没有艾德丽安了，直到也许她结婚的时候。这是因为青春期发生了，青少年时期发生了，你突然在家庭内部，在父母和孩子之间产生了一种紧张关系。

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.450
<v Ira Glass>So when there are kids in the films, they're cute and they're little and they're happy.
<v Ira Glass>所以当影片里有孩子时，他们很可爱，很小，很快乐。

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.840
<v Alan Berliner>All the time. All the time.
<v Alan Berliner>一直如此。一直如此。

00:03:48.840 --> 00:04:18.150
<v Ira Glass>Home movies are a family's way of preserving, forever, the thought, we were a nice family. We loved each other. Any feeling, any fact, about the family besides that fact, generally does not make it into the films. And that's as true, Alan Berliner says, of the black and white films that he's watched from the 1920s as the videos taken last month at his niece's birthday party.
<v Ira Glass>家庭录像是一个家庭永远保留这种想法的方式：我们是一个幸福的家庭。我们彼此相爱。除了这个事实之外，关于家庭的任何感受、任何事实，通常都不会出现在影片中。艾伦·伯林纳说，这不仅适用于他观看过的 1920 年代的黑白影片，也适用于上个月在他侄女生日派对上拍的录像。

00:04:18.150 --> 00:05:06.550
<v Ira Glass>Because all the images in everybody's home movies are the same, because the pictures are all the same, all the meaning comes from things that are not in the pictures, which makes them, I have to say, the perfect subject for a radio show. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, Home Movies, what they say in spite of themselves. We bring you five movies for radio in five acts, including stories from David Sedaris and Jonathan Goldstein, a look at the cinematic output of 12-year-old auteurs, and what the producers of America's Funniest Home Videos know about America that you don't. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>因为每个人家庭录像里的画面都是一样的，因为照片都一样，所有的意义都来自于画面之外的东西，这让我不得不说，它们是广播节目的完美主题。来自芝加哥 WBEZ，《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目是《家庭录像》，它们在不经意间诉说着什么。我们为您带来五幕广播电影，包括大卫·塞达里斯和乔纳森·戈德斯坦的故事，看看 12 岁的小导演们的电影作品，以及《全美最有趣家庭录像》的制作者们对美国了解而你不知道的事情。请继续收听。

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:42.060
<v Ira Glass>Act One, The Big Night. We thought that we should begin today's program with one of the classic, typical kinds of home movies, a video of a holiday meal, a family meal. All the relatives sitting around the table. I'm sure that you have seen these at some point from Thanksgivings or Christmases. There are not many videos from the Jewish New Year dinner, Rosh Hashanah, a big family event, one of the big Jewish holidays, partly because Jewish law prohibits using a video camera or any other machine on that holy day. That did not stop Jonathan Goldstein's family from filming one.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，大日子。我们想以一种经典、典型的家庭录像来开始今天的节目，一段节日晚餐，家庭聚餐的视频。所有的亲戚围坐在桌子旁。我相信你们在感恩节或圣诞节时看过这些。关于犹太新年的晚餐，赎罪日，一个大型家庭活动，犹太教的大节日之一，并没有多少视频，部分原因是犹太法律禁止在那个神圣的日子使用摄像机或任何机器。但这并没有阻止乔纳森·戈德斯坦的家人拍摄了其中一场。

00:05:42.060 --> 00:06:04.360
<v Jonathan Goldstein>When I was a teenager, the Rosh Hashanah tape was the first thing I'd show any new girlfriend. It was a primer on my family that I felt they would need to see in order to best understand me. In showing them the tape, it was like I wanted to make them understand that no matter how messed up I was, all things considered, I really could have been a lot worse. It's been about 10 years since I've looked at the tape.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当我还是个青少年时，那盘赎罪日的录像带是我会给任何新女友看的第一样东西。那是我家庭的入门指南，我觉得她们需要看这个才能更好地了解我。给她们看带子的时候，就好像我想让她们明白，无论我多么糟糕，总的来说，我其实可能更糟糕。距离我上次看那盘带子已经过去大约 10 年了。

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:05.540
<v Father>Are you filming me?
<v Father>你在拍我吗？

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:06.650
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Yeah.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>是啊。

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:09.110
<v Mother>Johnny, put that away, come on.
<v Mother>强尼，把它收起来，得了吧。

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:29.050
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The night begins with a feeling of expectation, everyone preparing for the big night. My mother clears stacks of laundry off the dining room table. With a great show of ceremony, she and my father moved the plastic-covered red velvet couch away from the wall and pull out the extra dining room table leaves from behind it. We'd only use the table twice a year.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那个夜晚以一种期待感开始，每个人都在为大日子做准备。我母亲把餐桌上的几堆衣物挪开。她带着极大的仪式感，和父亲把靠墙的塑料封套红色天鹅绒沙发移开，从后面拉出额外的餐桌板。我们一年只用两次那张桌子。

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:30.220
<v Father>All the way?
<v Father>一直到底了吗？

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:30.700
<v Mother>Yes.
<v Mother>是的。

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:45.440
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Later in the evening, people start to arrive. When I'd show this to girlfriends, I would freeze-frame on each of the cast and explain a bit about them. This is my uncle Lou, I'd say. On the night of his mother's death, he ate an entire brisket, so great was his grief.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那天晚上晚些时候，人们开始陆续到达。当我给女朋友看这些时，我会暂停每一位出场人物，解释一下他们。这是路易叔叔，我会说。在他母亲去世的那个晚上，他吃下了一整块牛胸肉，他的悲伤就是如此巨大。

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.820
<v Uncle Lou>Hi, kid. Say hi.
<v Uncle Lou>嗨，孩子。打个招呼。

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.850
<v Mother>Don't give me a close-up because my eyes are too ugly for close-ups.
<v Mother>别给我特写，因为我的眼睛太丑了，不适合特写。

00:06:50.850 --> 00:07:03.490
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Placing the camera up to my eye and squinting into it like that felt good, the way it covered up my face like a mask and stuffed the whole Yiddish circus of my family into a tidy, manageable, little frame that I could fade in and out at will.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>把摄像机放在眼边，像那样眯着眼看感觉很好，它像面具一样遮住了我的脸，把我家庭的整个犹太马戏团塞进了一个整洁、可控的小框架里，我可以随意淡入淡出。

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:06.160
<v Father>That camera has everything on it, everything.
<v Father>那台摄像机什么功能都有，什么都有。

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.210
<v Male Relative 1>Has it got a stand too?
<v Male Relative 1>它有支架吗？

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.970
<v Father>You named the one thing it doesn't have.
<v Father>你点出了它唯一没有的东西。

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:14.100
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Early on in the night, I filmed my mother in the kitchen.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那天晚上很早的时候，我拍了厨房里的母亲。

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.420
<v Mother>[SINGING]
<v Mother>[唱歌]

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:25.560
<v Jonathan Goldstein>She dances around and sings for the camera while preparing dinner. My grandmother walks in at one point and asks her where the Kleenex is, and my mother tells her to use toilet paper.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>她一边准备晚餐，一边绕着摄像机跳舞唱歌。外婆在某个时候走进来，问她纸巾在哪儿，母亲让她用卫生纸。

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.420
<v Mother>No, go use toilet paper. [SINGING]
<v Mother>不，去用卫生纸。[唱歌]

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:53.760
<v Jonathan Goldstein>In our house, whether you were blowing your nose, mopping up spilled pea soup, or bandaging a head injury, it was toilet paper. As a kid, I was embarrassed by this. Toilet paper. It sounded so personal. And pulling it out of my lunch bag to use as a napkin in the high school cafeteria brought just enough of my family bathroom to the table to make those around me lose their appetite.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>在我们家，不管是擦鼻涕、擦掉洒出的豌豆汤，还是包扎头部伤口，用的都是卫生纸。小时候我为此感到尴尬。卫生纸。听起来太私密了。把它从午餐袋里掏出来当餐巾纸在高中食堂用，带给周围人的家庭洗手间的气息足以让他们失去食欲。

00:07:53.760 --> 00:08:24.650
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My mother singing and dancing around the house was another point of embarrassment for me as a kid. I felt like watching her sing "Let's Get Physical" while cooking pancakes was a mild form of child abuse. Watching her now on the tape, I realize she was singing, not because she was crazy, but because she was actually happy. Both her parents were still alive, so she'd let herself act like someone's little kid sometimes. I haven't seen her sing like that in years.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>母亲在家绕着圈唱歌跳舞是我小时候另一个尴尬点。我觉得看她一边做煎饼一边唱《让我们动起来》是一种轻微的虐童行为。现在看着带子里的她，我意识到她唱歌不是因为她疯了，而是因为她真的很开心。她父母那时都还健在，所以她有时会让自己表现得像别人的小孩子一样。我已经好多年没见过她那样唱歌了。

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:35.080
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The presence of the video camera puts my great-aunt Simi in a time capsule-y kind of mood. She decides to tell this story about her son for posterity, so my father helps her shout the table down.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>摄像机的存在让西米姨婆有一种时光胶囊般的心情。她决定为了后代讲讲她儿子的故事，所以我父亲帮她压住桌子上的喧闹。

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.680
<v Father>Let Simi talk.
<v Father>让西米说。

00:08:36.680 --> 00:09:02.150
<v Aunt Simi>When Jeffrey was four years old, we went downtown. And we were caught in the rain waiting for the bus. So there was a Reichmann's store on St. Lawrence and St. Catherine, with a little hallway, so we went in to wait. And there was a display in the windows of lady's lingerie and everything. He looks in the window, and there's men and women shielding themselves from the rain. "Mommy, what's that?"
<v Aunt Simi>杰弗里四岁的时候，我们去市中心。我们在雨中等公交车。圣劳伦斯和圣凯瑟琳路口有一家莱希曼商店，带一个小走廊，所以我们进去等。橱窗里陈列着女士内衣之类的东西。他看着橱窗，有男男女女在躲雨。“妈妈，那是什么？”

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:12.670
<v Aunt Simi>I take a look, and I give his hand a jerk. I say, "Nothing." "Oh, you know. You know. You have it in your drawer." Guess what it was? A pair of falsies.
<v Aunt Simi>我看了一眼，拽了一下他的手。我说，“没什么。”“噢，你知道。你知道。你的抽屉里就有。”猜猜那是什么？一对义乳。

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:14.550
<v Male Relative 2>Oh, is that something else?
<v Male Relative 2>噢，那是别的什么吗？

00:09:14.550 --> 00:10:00.210
<v Jonathan Goldstein>When I was a teenager, right here is where I'd pause the tape. I would freeze-frame the image of my great-aunt Simi, mouth open, just about to pop in an olive. With that as my backdrop, I would tell the story of how, when I was a child, Simi would command me to pinch her buttocks. "You can't pinch it," she'd cry, "and that is because it is too tight. Go ahead." "Keep trying. Keep trying," she'd continue, her face all clenched like a fist, all determined to make her buttocks as hard as a cantaloupe. In her slippery, skin-tight polyester slacks, trying to get a piece of my aunt's ass was as elusive as pinching a helium-filled balloon. Now, watching the tape with friends a half-generation later, I'm not going to lie to you, that story still kills.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当我还是个青少年时，我就会在这儿暂停带子。我会定格西米姨婆的画面，她嘴巴张着，正要吃下一颗橄榄。以那为背景，我会讲我小时候的故事，西米会命令我去捏她的屁股。“你捏不动，”她会叫道，“那是因为它太紧了。来吧。”“继续试。继续试，”她会继续说，脸像拳头一样紧绷，决心要把她的屁股练得像哈密瓜一样硬。穿着她那滑溜溜、紧贴皮肤的涤纶长裤，想要抓住我姨妈的屁股就像捏住一个充氦气球一样难以捉摸。现在，在半代人之后和朋友们看这盘带子，我不会骗你，那个故事依然好笑得要命。

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:20.500
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Incidentally, I should also say that Simi was the first person who ever really felt me up. Bored at my uncle Harry's Shiva house, she decided to check and see what was in the front pockets of my pants. She uncovered gum wrappers, old movie stubs, and a surprising amount of toilet paper, which she emptied out onto the kitchen table in three glorious scoops that I later referred to as my real bar mitzvah.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>顺便说一句，我也该说西米是第一个真正摸我的人。在哈里叔叔的守丧期感到无聊时，她决定检查一下我裤子前兜里有什么。她翻出了口香糖纸、旧电影票根，还有数量惊人的卫生纸，她分三下壮观地掏出来放在餐桌上，我后来称之为我真正的受诫礼。

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.490
<v Female Relative 1>Who made this marble cake?
<v Female Relative 1>这大理石蛋糕是谁做的？

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:25.050
<v Mother>Steve and Lisa's brother-in-law's [UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Mother>史蒂夫和丽莎姐夫的[听不清]。

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:26.010
<v Male Relative 3>This is excellent.
<v Male Relative 3>这太棒了。

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:51.320
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I should probably also tell you that the entire Rosh Hashanah meal is consumed in 15 minutes flat. I mean the whole thing, the gefilte fish entree, the salad, the handmade coleslaw, the bean and barley soup, and the main course. 15 minutes. I remember the TV was on in the basement, and Jeopardy had just started. By the time it was Double Jeopardy, we were eating dessert. But then, paradoxically, dessert was something that lingered on and on.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我还应该告诉你们，整顿赎罪日大餐是在 15 分钟内吃完的。我是说整个过程，冻鱼前菜，沙拉，手工凉拌卷心菜，豆子大麦汤，还有主菜。15 分钟。我记得楼下的电视开着，《危险边缘》刚开始。等到《双倍危险边缘》时，我们已经在吃甜点了。但矛盾的是，甜点却持续了很久很久。

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:56.520
<v Male Relative 4>What are these? Moon cookies? Who made these moon cookies? They're out of this world.
<v Male Relative 4>这些是什么？月亮曲奇？谁做的这些月亮曲奇？简直太好吃了。

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:23.170
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My grandfather ate dessert alone in the basement, so he could watch baseball. My grandfather was of the mind that no one up there listened to him anyway, so he pretty much stopped trying. He spoke through his actions, random outrageous incomprehensible actions. Like if he was tired and wanted you to get out of his house, he would come out wearing just his boxer shorts and sit right down beside you on the couch. You would collect your things, and you would leave.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>外公独自在地下室吃甜点，这样他就能看棒球。外公觉得反正楼上没人听他的，所以他基本放弃了尝试。他通过行动说话，随机的、离谱的、不可理喻的行动。比如如果他累了，想让你离开他家，他就会只穿着平角内裤出来，直接坐在沙发上你旁边。你就会收拾东西，然后离开。

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:40.440
<v Jonathan Goldstein>This next part of the tape, of him and me in the basement, I never made jokes about. I was so embarrassed by my inability to make simple man-to-man chitchat with my grandfather that when screening the tape I would often just fast-forward through the whole scene because it pained me so. It was just around the whole Pete Rose gambling scandal.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>带子上接下来这一部分，我和他在地下室，我从来不开玩笑。我对自己无法和外公进行简单的成年人谈话感到如此尴尬，以至于在放映带子时，我经常直接快进掉整个场景，因为这让我太痛苦了。那正好是皮特·罗斯赌博丑闻期间。

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.870
<v Jonathan Goldstein>They showed him on the cover of Time, crying.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他们让他在《时代》周刊封面上哭泣。

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:45.000
<v Grandfather>That's what they call it? Crying?
<v Grandfather>他们管那叫什么？哭泣？

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.790
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Well, whatever it is. I'm sure he's very disappointed actually.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好吧，不管是什么。我相信他真的很失望。

00:11:49.790 --> 00:12:13.290
<v Ira Glass>This is what most of our conversations were like at the time. He would stare at the TV, and I would get all sweaty, trying my hardest to talk about anything I could think of that had to do with sports. Since I never watched them, nor did I play them, my talk revolved around the girlier aspects of sports, the scandals, the exorbitant pay raises, the poem I might have seen Muhammad Ali recite on The Mike Douglas Show. And all the while, my grandfather just sat there.
<v Ira Glass>这就是那时我们大部分对话的样子。他盯着电视，而我紧张得汗流浃背，竭尽全力想谈任何我能想到的跟体育有关的话题。因为我从不看体育比赛，也不参与，我的谈话内容围绕着体育中比较女性化的方面：丑闻、过高的薪水加成、我可能在迈克·道格拉斯秀上看到穆罕默德·阿里朗诵的诗。而在此期间，我外公只是坐在那儿。

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:13.920
<v Jonathan Goldstein>He was a legend.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他是传奇。

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:15.710
<v Grandfather>Hall-of-Famer.
<v Grandfather>名人堂成员。

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:20.200
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Well, they can't omit him from that. That's up to the critics, I think.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好吧，他们不能忽略他。那是评论家们的事，我想。

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:49.500
<v Ira Glass>I have no idea what the hell I'm talking about. Watching the video all these years later, actually 13 years after my grandfather's death, I realize in retrospect that this was around the time he was diagnosed with leukemia. No wonder he didn't want to talk. No wonder he went to the basement. At the time, except for my grandmother, he had told no one. Those kinds of personal things weren't ever talked about in the open. When we all got together, we argued about where the best place to buy a roast chicken was or what was on TV.
<v Ira Glass>我根本不知道我在胡说八道什么。多年以后看这段视频，实际上是在外公去世 13 年后，我事后才意识到那时正是他被诊断出患有白血病的时候。难怪他不想说话。难怪他要去地下室。当时，除了外婆，他没告诉任何人。那种私人的事情从来不在公开场合讨论。当我们聚在一起时，我们为了去哪儿买烤鸡最好或者电视上播什么而争论。

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.150
<v Male Relative 2>Well, did you see her today, Oprah, about them?
<v Male Relative 2>那么，你今天看奥普拉了吗，关于他们的？

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:51.620
<v Female Relative 2>No, they don't get big.
<v Female Relative 2>不，他们不会长大的。

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:54.680
<v Male Relative 2>This guy had a big one, and that guy has a small one.
<v Male Relative 2>那个人有一个大的，那个人有一个小的。

00:12:54.680 --> 00:13:30.680
<v Jonathan Goldstein>No one gets to finish a story. Conversation is like this verbal game of murderball, back and forth, everybody shouting over everybody else, nobody really listening to anybody. But it goes further than even that. Here's my mother and her aunt Simi sitting side by side on the living room couch with my grandmother, all of them kind of staring off into space after the huge dinner. Then suddenly, apropos to nothing, one of them starts singing "White Christmas." Then the other one, as though sitting on a different couch in a different universe, as if the thought had just arrived from out of the blue, pipes up with the exact same song, never looking at the other, never acknowledging she's there.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>没人能把故事讲完。谈话就像这种名为“谋杀球”的语言游戏，来来回回，每个人都在打断别人大喊大叫，没人真正听别人说话。但它甚至不仅限于此。这是我母亲和她的姨妈西米，还有我外婆坐在客厅沙发上，在丰盛的晚餐后都盯着虚空。然后突然，毫无征兆地，其中一个人开始唱《白色圣诞节》。然后另一个人，就像坐在不同宇宙的另一张沙发上，仿佛这个想法刚从天而降，也唱起了同一首歌，从来不看对方，也不承认对方在那儿。

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.890
<v Aunt Simi>[SINGING] I'm dreaming of a white Christmas--
<v Aunt Simi>[唱歌] 我梦想着一个白色圣诞节——

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:39.430
<v Mother>[SINGING] I'm dreaming of a white Christmas--
<v Mother>[唱歌] 我梦想着一个白色圣诞节——

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:40.920
<v Aunt Simi>[SINGING] --just like the ones--
<v Aunt Simi>[唱歌] ——就像那些——

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.400
<v Mother>[SINGING] --just like the ones we used to know.
<v Mother>[唱歌] ——就像那些我们曾经熟悉的。

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:43.530
<v Aunt Simi>[SINGING] --I used to know.
<v Aunt Simi>[唱歌] ——我曾经熟悉的。

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:56.680
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Let's not even get into the fact that, one, they're singing a Christmas carol in the middle of September, and two, it also happens to be the holiest night on the Jewish calendar.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我们甚至不用谈论这样一个事实：第一，他们在九月中旬唱圣诞颂歌；第二，那天正好是犹太历中最神圣的夜晚。

00:13:56.680 --> 00:14:41.140
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What all this chaos leads to, the logical climax in consequence of the entire evening, is when, in the middle of dinner, my father gets it into his head that right now, at that very instant, he was going to fix the loose knob on the front door. The doorknob had been loose for years at this point, and why he decided to fix it just then, to this day, he cannot say. So my father goes downstairs into the garage to get his toolbox, which, to be fair, isn't actually a literal toolbox, but a plastic lunch pail which contains a wrench, a pair of rusty pliers, a plastic 12-inch ruler, and a screwdriver. The camera casually pans past a wall of neatly-stacked spare toilet paper on the garage shelves. My uncle Lou decides to help.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>所有这一切混乱导致的结果，整晚逻辑上的高潮后果是，在晚餐进行到一半时，我父亲突发奇想，就在那一刻，他要去修前门那个松动的把手。门把手当时已经松动多年了，他为什么偏偏在那时决定修理它，直到今天他都说不上来。于是我父亲下楼去车库拿工具箱，说实话，那其实不是真正的工具箱，而是一个塑料饭盒，里面装着一把扳手、一把生锈的钳子、一把 12 英寸的塑料尺和一把螺丝刀。摄像机随意扫过车库架子上整齐堆放的备用卫生纸。路易叔叔决定帮忙。

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:43.840
<v Father>These screwdrivers are [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. No, that isn't the one I want.
<v Father>这些螺丝刀是[听不清]。不，那不是我要的那把。

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:45.870
<v Uncle Lou>Well, tighten the thing here.
<v Uncle Lou>好吧，把这东西拧紧。

00:14:45.870 --> 00:15:05.520
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Now, let me make this as clear as I can. When my father and Lou first started working on the doorknob, the time code in the corner of the video screen reads 6:27. They would only finish two and a half hours later, and almost all of which time my father spent on his knees turning the doorknob screw around and around, for the most part, in the same continuous direction.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>现在，让我尽量说清楚。我父亲和路易第一次开始修门把手时，视频屏幕角落的时间码显示是 6:27。他们两个半小时后才完成，而我父亲几乎所有时间都跪在地上，不断地转动门把手上的螺丝，大部分时间里，都是朝着同一个连续方向。

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:06.980
<v Father>Just a minute.
<v Father>稍等一下。

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:09.900
<v Uncle Lou>He's taking pictures with the thing.
<v Uncle Lou>他在用那东西拍照。

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.990
<v Father>I'm going to go down and get-- Johnny, please, excuse me.
<v Father>我要下去拿——强尼，拜托，失陪一下。

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:31.670
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The other guests sing more songs, try on clothes, exchange recipes, take third and fourth helpings of dessert. And through all of it, my dad turning and turning like a Jewish Lady Macbeth, one who's abandoned murder for home repair. At some point, people start to funnel out the door to go home.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>其他客人在唱更多的歌，试穿衣服，交流食谱，吃第三次、第四次甜点。而在这一切之中，我父亲像个犹太版的麦克白夫人一样转呀转，一个为了居家维修而放弃谋杀的麦克白夫人。在某个时刻，人们开始从门外疏散回家。

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:32.670
<v Female Relative 3>Lou, good night.
<v Female Relative 3>路易，晚安。

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:34.160
<v Female Relative 4>Good night.
<v Female Relative 4>晚安。

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:35.090
<v Female Relative 3>Buzz, take care.
<v Female Relative 3>巴兹，保重。

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:37.180
<v Buzz>OK, take care.
<v Buzz>好的，保重。

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:41.470
<v Jonathan Goldstein>They shuffle around his kneeling body and say goodbye, like none of this is at all strange.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他们在他跪着的身体周围走动并告别，好像这一切一点也不奇怪。

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:42.860
<v Buzz>Good night.
<v Buzz>晚安。

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:43.770
<v Mother>Bye-bye sweetheart.
<v Mother>再见，亲爱的。

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:54.050
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My father does not look up, still hypnotized by the turning of the screw. When I try to tear him  away from the job, he tells me he can't help it. It's an emergency.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我父亲没有抬头，依然被螺丝的转动催眠了。当我试图把他从工作中拉开时，他告诉我他没法帮忙。这是紧急情况。

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:56.470
<v Father>Is that turning? That's OK. That's turning.
<v Father>那是转了吗？没关系。那转了。

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:57.440
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Is that turning?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那是转了吗？

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:04.710
<v Father>That's turning.
<v Father>那是转了。

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:25.030
<v Jonathan Goldstein>So I thought this was the end of the whole thing. But having not seen the tape in over 10 years, I've forgotten that I also videoed the second night of Rosh Hashanah. So I keep watching, and then I see something that I have absolutely no memory of whatsoever. Sitting at the dining room table, sandwiched between my sister and grandmother, is this entire British family.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>所以我以为这就是整件事的结局了。但因为有 10 多年没看这盘带子了，我忘了我也拍了赎罪日的第二天。所以我继续看，然后看到了一些我完全没有记忆的东西。坐在餐桌旁，夹在我姐姐和外婆中间的，是这一整个英国家庭。

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:26.760
<v British Mother>Do you brush it after you wash it?
<v British Mother>你洗完头会梳它吗？

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.560
<v Mother>I brush it, and I put in big, big rollers.
<v Mother>我梳它，然后用大大的发卷。

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:46.630
<v Jonathan Goldstein>There's an older Englishman, well-groomed and proper, sitting there sipping wine and actually sporting an ascot. There's his wife, an animated woman who chain smokes Virginia Slims, and their daughter, a woman who actually tells anecdotes about current events and expresses genuine interest in my video making.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>有一个上了年纪的英国人，穿着考究，得体，坐在那里喝着酒，甚至还戴着领巾。还有他的妻子，一个抽着维吉尼亚纤细香烟的活泼女人，以及他们的女儿，一个讲述时事轶事并对我的摄像表达了真诚兴趣的女人。

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:54.740
<v British Daughter>Well, I know my father, what he did was he had a lot of eight-millimeter old movies from where I was growing up.
<v British Daughter>好吧，我了解我父亲，他所做的是他有很多 8 毫米的旧电影，记录了我成长过程中的点滴。

00:16:54.740 --> 00:17:04.860
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It's like they wandered off the set of a different home movie and into ours. And the strangest part of the whole thing is that everyone's acting like nothing unusual is going on.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>就好像他们从另一部家庭录像的片场溜进了我们这部。最奇怪的部分是每个人表现得好像什么不同寻常的事情都没发生一样。

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.300
<v Female Relative 5>Do you drink tea or coffee?
<v Female Relative 5>你喝茶还是咖啡？

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:21.630
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Tea. As well as drinking tea, we drink amaretto, and everyone is bumming cigarettes off the old British woman. All of a sudden, we're all having a grand time. Even my grandfather is. Instead of sitting in the basement, he's asking genial questions like:
<v Jonathan Goldstein>茶。除了喝茶，我们还喝苦杏酒，每个人都在蹭那位老英国女人的烟抽。突然之间，我们都玩得很开心。连我外公也是。他不再坐在地下室，而是问一些和蔼的问题，比如：

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.120
<v Grandfather>Who made this beautiful apple cake?
<v Grandfather>这漂亮的苹果蛋糕是谁做的？

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:40.500
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It turns out the British family were my Uncle Melvin's in-laws, who were in from out of town. The evening plays out like that old Disney movie, Song of the South . The British are what appear to be real normal people, and my family are like the cartoon bluebirds fluttering around them, trying in earnest to keep them entertained.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>原来那个英国家庭是我梅尔文叔叔的亲家，是从外地来的。那个夜晚的展开就像那部古老的迪士尼电影《南方之歌》。英国人看起来是真正正常的人，而我的家人就像环绕在他们周围的卡通蓝知更鸟，认真地试图让他们保持娱乐。

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:44.090
<v Female Relative 5>I use one [? schmuck ?] per week. Last year, I used to use 10 a day.
<v Female Relative 5>我每周用一个[听不清]。去年，我一天用 10 个。

00:17:44.090 --> 00:18:09.980
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I don't remember filming this, but I'm certain I identified with the normal people, not with the cartoon birds. I remember looking through the camera at my family and feeling like I was a million miles away, like I was looking into the large end of a telescope. It made me feel like less a part of them, like more of just an observer. But then there's one moment near the end of the night, where I hand the video camera over to my grandmother, a total old-country technophobe, so she could film me.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我不记得拍这个了，但我肯定我认同那些正常人，而不是卡通鸟。我记得透过摄像机看着我的家人，感觉就像我在一百万英里之外，就像我透过望远镜的大端向里看。这让我觉得自己不像他们的一部分，更像是一个观察者。但就在夜晚快结束时，有那么一刻，我把摄像机交给了外婆，一个完全的老派科技恐惧症患者，让她拍我。

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:12.560
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Now you're filming me, right now. You see?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>现在你在拍我，就在现在。你看？

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:14.840
<v Grandmother>I have to close--
<v Grandmother>我必须关掉——

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:18.400
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And when you want to stop it, you press the red button again.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当你想要停止时，再按一次红色按钮。

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:21.110
<v Grandmother>OK. Go-- yeah, move around a bit.
<v Grandmother>好的。开始——是的，走动一下。

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:43.430
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And there I am, moving around with my tapered pants, jelly bracelets, and long moussed-up '80s hair, which, in retrospect, actually looked a lot like my mother's perm. Watching the video, I see the way my grandfather gritted his teeth when he stretched, or how he did this thing with his shoulders when he drank something that was too hot, and it's just the way I do it. Here I am, comparing wrist size with my father.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>然后就是我，穿着锥形裤，戴着果冻手镯，留着长长的 80 年代摩丝发型，回想起来，看起来确实很像我母亲的烫发。看着视频，我看到外公伸懒腰时紧咬牙关的样子，或者他喝太烫的东西时耸肩的样子，和我一模一样。这就是我，和我父亲比较手腕大小。

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:57.190
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Look at this. It runs in the family maybe. Oh, no, you've got a very thick wrist. That's a thick wrist. I think on Mommy's side of the family, we have thin wrists. I told you, I've got very thin wrists.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>看这个。也许是遗传。噢，不，你的手腕很粗。那是粗手腕。我想在妈那边的家族里，我们手腕很细。我告诉过你，我的手腕很细。

00:18:57.190 --> 00:19:03.250
<v Ira Glass>Posed against the British family or any other family since, there's no question as to what family I belong to.
<v Ira Glass>与英国家庭或之后任何其他家庭相比，毫无疑问我属于哪个家庭。

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:06.690
<v Jonathan Goldstein>He's funny, Jackie Mason. I'm not nuts about the show though. Me neither.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他很有趣，杰基·梅森。不过我不怎么喜欢这个秀。我也不。

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:08.020
<v Father>Chicken soup? Oh yeah, it's good.
<v Father>鸡汤？噢，是啊，不错。

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:08.460
<v Female Relative 5>I got news for you.
<v Female Relative 5>我有消息告诉你。

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:09.610
<v Father>But he's not Jackie Mason.
<v Father>但他不是杰基·梅森。

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:09.950
<v Female Relative 5>I don't like him and--
<v Female Relative 5>我不喜欢他，而且——

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:10.640
<v Father>Jackie Mason is--
<v Father>杰基·梅森是——

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:16.830
<v Ira Glass>Jonathan Goldstein is the host of the radio show Wiretap on the CBC and the author of the book Ladies And Gentlemen, The Bible!
<v Ira Glass>乔纳森·戈德斯坦是加拿大广播公司《窃听》广播节目的主持人，也是《女士们先生们，圣经！》一书的作者。

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:37.560
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "YOU TALK TO MUCH" BY RUN-DMC]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 跑-DMC的《你们话太多》]

00:19:37.560 --> 00:20:11.980
<v Ira Glass>Act  Two, The Kids Stay In The Picture. If children ran Hollywood, what sort of films would they make? Well, because of video cameras, we can actually answer that question. There are 11-year-old auteurs. When he was a little kid, Darren Stein organized the kids on a little suburban cul-de-sac where he lived in Encino, California to make movies. They made dozens of films. They shot all the time, over the course of years. Darren says that he remembers wanting to get control of the video camera and make a movie from the moment that his dad first brought a camera home. He was seven years old, and he quickly invented a film for everyone to do.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，孩子们留在画面里。如果孩子们管理好莱坞，他们会制作什么样的电影？好吧，因为有摄像机，我们实际上可以回答这个问题。这有 11 岁的小导演。当他还是个小孩子时，达伦·斯坦组织了他住在加州恩西诺小郊区尽头的小区里的孩子们制作电影。他们制作了几十部影片。他们一直都在拍，历经多年。达伦说，他记得从他爸爸第一次带回家摄像机的那一刻起，他就想控制摄像机并制作一部电影。他当时七岁，很快就发明了一部电影让大家一起演。

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:24.430
<v Darren Stein>It was called Crazy News. And it was basically a newscast that was supposed to be crazy. And my dad was filming it, because at the time, I was seven or eight years old. So I wasn't allowed, nor could I probably hold the camera.
<v Darren Stein>它叫《疯狂新闻》。基本上是一档应该很疯狂的新闻广播。我爸爸在拍，因为那时我才七八岁。所以我没被允许，大概也没法拿住摄像机。

00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:26.170
<v Ira Glass>And you're in a rec room or something.
<v Ira Glass>然后你们在娱乐室之类的地方。

00:20:26.170 --> 00:20:46.590
<v Darren Stein>Yeah. We're in the den of the house. We're sitting on the couches. I had my neighborhood kids in a thing. It was me, and my next-door neighbor, Justin Satinover, and his sister, Lisa Satinover. My brother, Evan. Evan was probably five. He's three years younger than me. He's this tiny little kid wearing a little Batman shirt. And it begins with the camera on my brother, and he says:
<v Darren Stein>是的。我们在家里的书房。我们坐在沙发上。我把邻居家的孩子叫来一起拍。有我、隔壁邻居贾斯汀·萨蒂诺弗和他的妹妹丽莎·萨蒂诺弗。还有我弟弟埃文。埃文大概五岁。他比我小三岁。他是个穿着蝙蝠侠小衬衫的小不点。视频开头摄像机对着我弟弟，他说：

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:50.320
<v Evan Stein>[GIGGLING] Hello, ladies and gentlemen.
<v Evan Stein>[咯咯笑] 大家好，女士们先生们。

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:53.870
<v Young Darren Stein>Stop. Stop.
<v Young Darren Stein>停。停。

00:20:53.870 --> 00:21:00.380
<v Darren Stein>And first of all, Evan couldn't get his line right, so that took seven or eight times, which was incredibly frustrating to me. Because I was very serious about these things.
<v Darren Stein>首先，埃文台词说不对，那用了七八次，这让我感到非常沮丧。因为我对待这些事情非常严肃。

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:07.450
<v Evan Stein>[GIGGLING] Hello, ladies and gentlemen. This-- I forgot.
<v Evan Stein>[咯咯笑] 大家好，女士们先生们。这个——我忘了。

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:09.390
<v Darren Stein>I was so frustrated.
<v Darren Stein>我太沮丧了。

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:13.890
<v Young Darren Stein>Forget it. Stop it.
<v Young Darren Stein>算了。停下。

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:14.860
<v Justin Satinover>Now say it, Evan.
<v Justin Satinover>现在说，埃文。

00:21:14.860 --> 00:21:17.750
<v Young Darren Stein>No. I don't want this, daddy.
<v Young Darren Stein>不。我不要这样，爸爸。

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:21.140
<v Evan Stein>Hello, everybody. This is Crazy News. And now weather.
<v Evan Stein>大家好。这是《疯狂新闻》。现在是天气预报。

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:26.610
<v Darren Stein>And then it goes to me. And finally, I'm thrilled because I get to talk.
<v Darren Stein>然后轮到我。最后，我很激动，因为轮到我说话了。

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:38.360
<v Young Darren Stein>Hello, my name is Darren Stein from Crazy News. Now, in Arizona, it is so cold, it is snowing. Can you believe that? It's hot in Arizona.
<v Young Darren Stein>你好，我是来自《疯狂新闻》的达伦·斯坦。现在，在亚利桑那州，天气太冷了，正在下雪。你相信吗？亚利桑那州天气很热。

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:56.500
<v Darren Stein>So I was going on and on And I was saying this and that, and then the camera drifts away from me to this girl, Lisa. And she's sort of staring at the camera, looking at herself on TV, happy in that moment to have it on her. And I just flipped out because it wasn't the way I had planned it.
<v Darren Stein>所以我一直在讲个不停，我在说这个那个，然后摄像机从我身上移到了这个叫丽莎的女孩身上。她正盯着摄像机看，在电视上看着自己，那一刻很高兴能出镜。我立刻就炸毛了，因为这和我计划的不一样。

00:21:56.500 --> 00:22:01.410
<v Young Darren Stein>I'm not doing it. Forget it, God dang it.
<v Young Darren Stein>我不演了。算了，该死的。

