<v Male Child 5>Is this the end of the world? What would happen if this happened to the president? Will the world turn out gay? Stay tuned next week when Darren Stein Productions present:
<v Male Child 5>这是世界末日吗？如果这发生在总统身上会怎样？世界会变成同性恋吗？下周请继续关注，达伦·斯坦制片公司呈献：

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:47.600
<v Children>Gay as a Whistle.
<v Children>《像哨子一样直》。

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:55.730
<v Ira Glass>What weird about it is that it's simultaneously gay-loving and homophobic at the same time.
<v Ira Glass>奇怪的是它同时既拥护同性恋又恐同。

00:28:55.730 --> 00:29:08.440
<v Darren Stein>Well, because that's how kids are. I think that's how young gay kids are because you love yourself, but you hate yourself at the same time. What better way not to be gay than to make fun of them and to demonize them?
<v Darren Stein>好吧，因为小孩子就是这样。我认为年轻的同性恋孩子就是这样，因为你爱自己，但同时又恨自己。还有什么比嘲笑他们、妖魔化他们更能让自己不成为同性恋的方法呢？

00:29:08.440 --> 00:29:32.490
<v Adam Schell>I remember-- it's actually funny. If you watch it, at the very end, Evan and I come up. We're on the football team. And Allen turns us gay with the thing, and then we skip off together. And I remember you see me grab Evan and hug him. And I remember everybody else teasing me about that constantly, being like, oh, you must be gay because look what you did there. Look what you did.
<v Adam Schell>我记得——其实挺好笑的。如果你看它，最后埃文和我出现。我们是足球队的。艾伦用那东西把我们变成了同性恋，然后我们一起跳着跑开了。我记得你看到我抓住埃文并拥抱他。我记得其他人不断拿这事取笑我，比如，噢，你肯定是同性恋，看你刚才做了什么。看你做了什么。

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:36.680
<v Adam Schell>And to me, I was like, I'm playing the part. That's what I was supposed to do.
<v Adam Schell>对我来说，我当时想的是，我是在演戏。我本就该那么做。

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:38.350
<v Ira Glass>I am an actor.
<v Ira Glass>我是一个演员。

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:51.220
<v Adam Schell>Exactly. I'm an actor. I'm playing the part. I'm getting into this role. And I don't think there was ever a moment when somebody wasn't being picked on, whether it was Michael, me, Evan, Mark.
<v Adam Schell>没错。我是一个演员。我在演戏。我正在进入这个角色。我不认为有任何时候没人被欺负，无论是迈克尔、我、埃文还是马克。

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:54.920
<v Ira Glass>I noticed you don't list Darren in that little list.
<v Ira Glass>我注意到你没把达伦列在那份名单里。

00:29:54.920 --> 00:30:02.440
<v Adam Schell>Darren was never picked on. Darren was always the one doing the picking. Because if he started picking on somebody else, then he was safe.
<v Adam Schell>达伦从来没被欺负过。达伦总是那个欺负人的人。因为如果他开始欺负别人，那么他就安全了。

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:07.430
<v Ira Glass>You also made a musical called I Have No Friends .
<v Ira Glass>你还拍了一部叫《我没有朋友》的音乐剧。

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:16.710
<v Darren Stein>Yeah. I went to an all boys junior high/high school. And these kids just did not know what to make of me. And I got made fun of a lot.
<v Darren Stein>是的。我上了一所全男生的初中/高中。这些孩子根本不知道拿我怎么办。我经常被嘲笑。

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:18.090
<v Ira Glass>And you had no friends?
<v Ira Glass>你没有朋友？

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:30.440
<v Darren Stein>You have to understand something. When I was in sixth grade at this public school, I was president of my class. I was just used to being really loved by people and popular. And so suddenly, I wasn't anymore.
<v Darren Stein>你得理解一件事。我在公立学校上六年级时，我是班长。我习惯了被大家喜爱和欢迎。所以突然间，我不再是了。

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:33.360
<v Ira Glass>Right, and everybody hit adolescence. And you're the gay-seeming kid.
<v Ira Glass>对，大家进入了青春期。而你是那个看起来像同性恋的孩子。

00:30:33.360 --> 00:30:53.460
<v Darren Stein>Yeah, and I was completely gay and awkward and chunky and acne, and it was the whole-- and so the street was completely my escape from that all. It was my insulated world where I called the shots. But I Have No Friends was never consciously about me when I made that.
<v Darren Stein>是啊，我完全是个同性恋，笨拙、胖乎乎、长痘痘，那是整个——所以那条街完全是我逃避这一切的出口。那是我与世隔绝的世界，我说了算。《我没有朋友》在我制作它时，从来没想过是关于我自己的。

00:30:53.460 --> 00:31:02.920
<v Male Child 6>[SINGING] I have no friends. Every day at school I sit alone at lunch. I have no friends.
<v Male Child 6>[唱歌] 我没有朋友。每天在学校吃午饭我都独自坐着。我没有朋友。

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:12.640
<v Adam Schell>Darren didn't have friends. I think, as long as I can remember, Darren maybe had three or four friends throughout most of high school and junior high.
<v Adam Schell>达伦没有朋友。我认为，只要我记得，达伦在整个高中和初中期间可能只有三四个朋友。

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:14.080
<v Ira Glass>Outside the group, you mean.
<v Ira Glass>我是指在这个群体之外。

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:15.200
<v Adam Schell>Yeah, outside the group.
<v Adam Schell>是啊，在这个群体之外。

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:22.950
<v Male Child 6>[SINGING] I have no friends. I have no friends.
<v Male Child 6>[唱歌] 我没有朋友。我没有朋友。

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:36.400
<v Ira Glass>You've, as an adult, made two feature films, one called Jawbreaker, one called Sparkler. Did you feel that you had more freedom to express yourself when you were 11 and 12 making movies?
<v Ira Glass>你成年后制作了两部长片，一部叫《烂苹果》，一部叫《火花塞》。当你 11、12 岁拍电影时，你觉得表达自己的自由度更大吗？

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:45.410
<v Darren Stein>Unquestionably. When you're a kid, it comes straight from your id onto the film.
<v Darren Stein>毫无疑问。当你是个孩子时，它直接从你的本我流露到影片中。

00:31:45.410 --> 00:31:53.180
<v Ira Glass>Darren Stein and Adam Schell have collected their own movies and created a documentary about them called Put the Camera On Me . It's available on DVD.
<v Ira Glass>达伦·斯坦和亚当·谢尔收集了他们自己的电影，并创作了一部关于它们的纪录片叫《把摄像机对准我》。它可以在 DVD 上买到。

00:31:53.180 --> 00:32:09.960
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, when America just leaves the camera running, what does it see? And a Hollywood teen movie plot comes to a home movie. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，当美国人只是让摄像机一直开着，它会看到什么？一个好莱坞青春片情节进入了家庭录像。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播和国际公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:25.080
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, home movies and the stories they tell, intentionally and unintentionally. We've arrived at Act Three of our program.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们会选择一个主题，为您带来各种关于该主题的不同故事。今天的节目是《家庭录像》，以及它们有意或无意讲述的故事。我们进入了节目的第三幕。

00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:55.790
<v Ira Glass>Back when home movies were actually movies, real movies on real film, they were expensive to make. The cameras were hard to use. You actually had to know how to work a camera pretty well. You'd run out of film in just three minutes. So most people really did restrict themselves to filming celebrations, babies, and vacations. But now that video cameras and videotape are so cheap, relatively speaking, people just leave the camera running for hours. And nobody knows this better than the producers of America's Funniest Home Videos, which has an archive of half a million video clips.
<v Ira Glass>回到家庭录像是真正的电影、胶片上真正的电影的时代，制作它们很昂贵。摄像机很难用。你实际上必须相当了解怎么操作摄像机。你三分钟内胶片就用完了。所以大多数人确实限制自己只拍摄庆典、婴儿和假期。但现在摄像机和录像带如此便宜，相对而言，人们只是让摄像机开着好几个小时。没有人比《全美最有趣家庭录像》的制作人更了解这一点，它拥有 50 万个视频片段的档案。

00:32:55.790 --> 00:33:11.170
<v Ira Glass>Todd Thicke is the co-executive producer of the show. Trace Beaulieu and Mike Palleschi have been writers on the program. They each watch hundreds of home video clips each week. They say that they have seen video where people just set up a tripod and film themselves watching TV. They have no idea why.
<v Ira Glass>托德·瑟克是该节目的联合执行制片人。特蕾斯·博利厄和迈克·帕莱斯基一直是节目的编剧。他们每个人每周观看数百个家庭视频片段。他们说，他们见过人们架起三脚架拍摄自己看电视的视频。他们根本不知道为什么。

00:33:11.170 --> 00:33:13.300
<v Todd Thicke>A lot of kids on stage.
<v Todd Thicke>舞台上有很多孩子。

00:33:13.300 --> 00:33:28.100
<v Mike Palleschi>People golfing because they want to get their swing down. So that's why we have a million people losing their drivers. And we also see a Little League baseball game. We get a lot of the kid walking off the field saying, I gotta go pee, Mommy, and stuff like that.
<v Mike Palleschi>人们打高尔夫球因为他们想练好挥杆。所以这就是为什么我们有一百万人在丢失发球杆。我们也看到过小联盟棒球赛。我们看到很多孩子走出赛场说，我要去嘘嘘，妈妈，之类的东西。

00:33:28.100 --> 00:33:33.480
<v Todd Thicke>Then kids sleeping on toilets. Parents universally think their child sleeping on the toilet is funny.
<v Todd Thicke>然后是孩子们在马桶上睡觉。父母普遍认为自己的孩子在马桶上睡觉很有趣。

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:46.930
<v Ira Glass>And when somebody has a video of, for example-- I've seen videos on the show of somebody trying to change a lightbulb and then a mishap happening. How do you explain the fact that they're filming the changing of a light bulb?
<v Ira Glass>当有人拥有例如——我在节目中看过有人试图换灯泡然后发生事故的视频。你如何解释他们拍摄换灯泡这件事？

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:55.780
<v Todd Thicke>They don't lead very interesting lives? That's a highlight? Trace, why did they tape themselves changing a lightbulb?
<v Todd Thicke>他们过得并不有趣？那是亮点？特蕾斯，他们为什么拍自己换灯泡？

00:33:55.780 --> 00:33:58.380
<v Trace Beaulieu>Because they don't have kids and their cat is boring.
<v Trace Beaulieu>因为他们没有孩子，而且他们的猫很无聊。

00:33:58.380 --> 00:33:59.830
<v Todd Thicke>And they got a camera.
<v Todd Thicke>而且他们有摄像机。

00:33:59.830 --> 00:34:05.220
<v Ira Glass>And is it your impression that there are a lot of people out there filming all the time?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得外面有很多人一直在拍摄吗？

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:14.450
<v Todd Thicke>I think that that is fairly prevalent out there. I think if people have cameras, they're shooting stuff. We sometimes say, what are they shooting here? What did they have the camera set up for?
<v Todd Thicke>我认为这种情况相当普遍。我觉得如果人们有摄像机，他们就在拍东西。我们有时会说，他们在这儿拍什么呢？他们架起摄像机是为了什么？

00:34:14.450 --> 00:34:32.290
<v Trace Beaulieu>Sometimes we've seen people videotaping their sheetrocking. It's like, why are they taping this? And then someone will fall through a ceiling. But you see one, and you go, OK, that's an anomaly. And then you see 20 guys taping themselves sheetrocking.
<v Trace Beaulieu>有时我们看到人们拍摄他们装石膏板的过程。就像，他们为什么拍这个？然后有人会从天花板上掉下来。但你看到一个，你会想，好吧，那是异常情况。然后你看到 20 个人拍摄自己装石膏板。

00:34:32.290 --> 00:34:40.470
<v Todd Thicke>I think you tape what you got. If that's your life and that's what you do-- if you're Cameron Diaz, you're at a premier. And if you're sheetrocking--
<v Todd Thicke>我觉得你拍你有的东西。如果那是你的生活，那是你做的事——如果你是卡梅隆·迪亚兹，你在首映礼上。如果你是在装石膏板——

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:48.230
<v Mike Palleschi>There was a guy, yesterday, pulling his cactus out alongside of the pool. And then he fell into the pool. We thought, did he set that up?
<v Mike Palleschi>昨天有个家伙在泳池边拔仙人掌。然后他掉进了泳池。我们心想，那是他们安排好的吗？

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:48.740
<v Todd Thicke>Set up.
<v Todd Thicke>安排好的。

00:34:48.740 --> 00:34:51.710
<v Mike Palleschi>Or did they really say, we've got to go film Dad taking the cactus out?
<v Mike Palleschi>还是他们真的说，我们得去拍爸爸拔仙人掌？

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:55.390
<v Todd Thicke>It was a set up. We can spot the setups all the time.
<v Todd Thicke>那是安排好的。我们总能一眼看穿这些安排。

00:34:55.390 --> 00:34:59.880
<v Ira Glass>Aha. Now, that's very interesting. What's the tip off that it's a set up? What are the tricks? How can you tell?
<v Ira Glass>啊哈。那太有趣了。安排的提示是什么？有什么技巧？你怎么看出来的？

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:09.520
<v Trace Beaulieu>Camera placement is a big tip off. They have the shot. Normally, they don't get the shot. The camera's moving around, and they miss the birthday cake burning someone.
<v Trace Beaulieu>摄像机位置是个大提示。他们得到了镜头。通常，他们拍不到。摄像机到处晃，他们错过了生日蛋糕烧到人的镜头。

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:23.830
<v Todd Thicke>Yeah. Usually when the moment happens, when you're taping something and something happens, you drop the camera, just for a split second or something, or you take your eye off it, and the shot jerks away. But if you hold that shot steady, there was no surprise to you.
<v Todd Thicke>是啊。通常当时刻发生时，当你拍东西发生什么事情时，你会扔掉摄像机，哪怕只是一瞬间，或者你视线离开了它，镜头就会猛地移开。但如果你能保持那个镜头稳定，说明这事对你来说并不意外。

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:25.290
<v Ira Glass>I never would have thought of that.
<v Ira Glass>我从来没想过这一点。

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:30.280
<v Trace Beaulieu>I think it's an involuntary reaction. But not enough to help anyone.
<v Trace Beaulieu>我认为这是一种非自愿反应。但不足以帮助任何人。

00:35:30.280 --> 00:35:34.480
<v Ira Glass>Does everybody's family seem the same, when you watch one of these videos after another?
<v Ira Glass>当你一个接一个看这些视频时，每个家庭看起来都一样吗？

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:57.640
<v Mike Palleschi>Yes and no. I mean, you see at the weddings when they pass the camera around and the microphone, and people say things into the camera, we find rich, poor, black, or white, there's always the person who puts the microphone up to their ear like a telephone. Or we had an old lady take the microphone and said, I'll have the chicken. It seems like, no matter what you are, there's always that person at your wedding.
<v Mike Palleschi>是也不是。我是说，你看婚礼时他们传递摄像机和麦克风，人们对着摄像机说话，我们发现无论是富人、穷人、黑人还是白人，总有那个人像打电话一样把麦克风贴在耳朵边。或者我们有位老太太接过麦克风说，我要吃鸡肉。看起来无论你是谁，婚礼上总有那个人。

00:35:57.640 --> 00:35:58.670
<v Ira Glass>Who will make that joke.
<v Ira Glass>谁会开那个玩笑。

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:02.660
<v Mike Palleschi>And every wedding has that one guy who really can't dance.
<v Mike Palleschi>而且每个婚礼都有那个确实不会跳舞的家伙。

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:12.120
<v Todd Thicke>But around the kitchen and the den when they're hanging out with the kids playing on the floor, everybody seems like, it's a nice country out there. Doesn't most of it seem-- in people's homes?
<v Todd Thicke>但在厨房和书房，当他们和在地上玩耍的孩子们待在一起时，每个人看起来都像，那是个美好的国家。大部分看起来不都像——在人们家里吗？

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:14.660
<v Ira Glass>Do you guys shoot home videos?
<v Ira Glass>你们拍家庭录像吗？

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:38.860
<v Mike Palleschi>I don't. But I did just get engaged. And I've seen more weddings, I think, than anybody. And my fiance couldn't understand why I was so nervous. And I had to explain to her, well, after two years of watching people catch on fire, throw up at the altar, pass out, and all these horrible things, people letting the doves go, and they poop on the bride, I was little squeamish about a wedding in general.
<v Mike Palleschi>我不拍。但我刚订婚。我觉得我看过的婚礼比任何人都多。我未婚妻不明白为什么我那么紧张。我不得不向她解释，好吧，看了两年人们着火、在祭坛上呕吐、昏倒，以及所有这些可怕的事情，人们放走鸽子，它们却拉在新娘身上，我对一般婚礼有点敏感。

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:43.100
<v Ira Glass>Right. A wedding just seems like a series of possible accidents waiting to happen.
<v Ira Glass>对。婚礼看起来就像一系列等待发生的事故。

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:47.530
<v Mike Palleschi>Well, I've learned you don't to have open flame near the veils.
<v Mike Palleschi>好吧，我学会了头纱附近不要有明火。

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:48.970
<v Todd Thicke>The father's pants.
<v Todd Thicke>父亲的裤子。

00:36:48.970 --> 00:37:10.240
<v Mike Palleschi>Yes, all the old men must have their belts securely fastened around their waists. And no comedy at the altar. We've seen a lot of guys who-- oh, I can't find the ring. And then the UPS guy will run in the front. They've made the most special moment of their life into a Lucy skit.
<v Mike Palleschi>是的，所有的老男人都必须把皮带紧紧扣在腰上。祭坛上不要有喜剧。我们看过很多家伙——噢，我找不到戒指了。然后 UPS 的人会跑进前场。他们把自己一生中最特殊的时刻变成了露西的短剧。

00:37:10.240 --> 00:37:24.340
<v Ira Glass>Mike Palleschi, Todd Thicke, and Trace Beaulieu of America's Funniest Home Videos . You can watch that TV show on the ABC television network Sunday nights at 7:00, 6:00 Central. That was such an official-sounding thing to say on the radio. I've never said anything like that in my life.
<v Ira Glass>迈克·帕莱斯基、托德·瑟克和《全美最有趣家庭录像》的特蕾斯·博利厄。你可以在美国广播公司电视网每周日晚上 7:00，中部时间 6:00 收看该电视节目。那在广播里听起来太官方了。我这辈子从来没说过那样的话。

00:37:24.340 --> 00:37:53.040
<v Ira Glass>Well, Act Four. The Cinema Of Upward Mobility. The plot line of a home movie is usually more home than movie. These are not usually, as you may gather, the most cinematic stories being told. When she was a teenager, Susan Burton tried to do something that you see many teenagers attempt in teen movies, and she actually pulled it off. As luck would have it, the effort was caught on video, and we offer it here as a kind of alternative to the Hollywood version of the story. Her family's videos, I should say, begin inauspiciously enough.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，第四幕。向上流动的电影。家庭录像的情节通常是家庭多于电影。正如你们可能体会到的，这些通常不是最电影化的故事。当她还是青少年时，苏珊·伯顿试图做你在青少年电影中经常看到的许多青少年尝试的事情，而且她真的成功了。幸运的是，这段努力被视频拍了下来，我们把它作为该故事好莱坞版本的一种替代品放在这里。我应该说，她家庭的录像带开始得还算祥和。

00:37:53.040 --> 00:37:58.450
<v Susan Burton>My dad videotaped a lot of things, all the typical stuff. My little sister Betsy's preschool dance recital.
<v Susan Burton>我爸爸录了很多东西，所有典型的玩意儿。我小妹妹贝齐的学前班舞蹈表演。

00:37:58.450 --> 00:38:02.200
<v Children>[SINGING] Rubber ducky, you're the one.
<v Children>[唱歌] 橡皮鸭子，你就是那个。

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:04.680
<v Susan Burton>My solo in the fourth grade spring choral concert.
<v Susan Burton>我在四年级春季合唱音乐会上的独唱。

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:13.010
<v Susan Burton>[SINGING] The hills are alive with the sound of music.
<v Susan Burton>[唱歌] 群山因音乐之声而生机勃勃。

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:13.870
<v Ira Glass>Birthday dinners.
<v Ira Glass>生日晚餐。

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:26.020
<v Father>[SINGING] Happy birthday dear Susan. Happy birthday to you.
<v Father>[唱歌] 生日快乐，亲爱的苏珊。祝你生日快乐。

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:30.910
<v Susan Burton>Holidays like Easter Sunday, when we hunted for eggs the Bunny hid on the first floor of our house.
<v Susan Burton>像复活节星期天这样的节日，我们在房子的一楼寻找兔子藏起来的蛋。

00:38:30.910 --> 00:38:36.260
<v Betsy Burton>I found one in the bathroom. You know what? I found one in the bathroom.
<v Betsy Burton>我在洗手间发现了一个。你知道吗？我在洗手间发现了一个。

00:38:36.260 --> 00:38:37.810
<v Father>Was it in the toilet?
<v Father>它在马桶里吗？

00:38:37.810 --> 00:38:38.800
<v Betsy Burton>No.
<v Betsy Burton>没有。

00:38:38.800 --> 00:38:41.000
<v Father>I just wanted to make sure you found an egg.
<v Father>我只是想确保你找到了一个蛋。

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:43.090
<v Susan Burton>I found an egg.
<v Susan Burton>我找到了一个蛋。

00:38:43.090 --> 00:38:54.490
<v Ira Glass>Until two weeks ago, I hadn't watched these videos for years. When I put them in the VCR, I couldn't believe that the world could exist like this again, that it could just pop up like a stage set, my parents still married, all of us together in our old house.
<v Ira Glass>直到两周前，我已经好多年没看这些录像了。当我把它们放进录像机时，我无法相信世界能再次这样存在，它可以像舞台布景一样突然冒出来，我的父母依然结婚，我们所有人依然聚在我们老房子里。

00:38:54.490 --> 00:38:59.150
<v Mother>OK, why don't you guys all get next to Daddy? It's probably not good for me to be out in the dark, is it?
<v Mother>好的，为什么你们不都到爸爸身边去呢？我在黑暗中可能不太好，是吗？

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:00.080
<v Father>No, it's fine.
<v Father>不，没关系。

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:22.570
<v Susan Burton>When I was little and planning on becoming famous, I always used to imagine that my house would be turned into a museum of my life, an exact reproduction of my seat at the dining room table, the order of the dolls in a row on my cedar chest, the Ramona book marked at the place I'd stopped before going to bed. Having these videos is like buying a ticket and opening our yellow front door and taking the tour.
<v Susan Burton>小时候计划成名时，我总是想象我的家会变成我生活的博物馆，我餐桌座位的精确复制品，香柏木箱上娃娃的排列顺序，我在去睡觉前标记好的拉蒙娜书。拥有这些录像就像买了一张票，打开我们黄色的前门，开始参观。

00:39:22.570 --> 00:39:31.800
<v Susan Burton>When so much of your childhood is documented, there are going to be moments you wish had been left out. For me, that moment is an entire era, middle school. It begins with a hike up Baxter Mountain.
<v Susan Burton>当你童年的很大一部分被记录下来时，总会有你希望被删掉的时刻。对我来说，那个时刻是一个完整的时代：初中。它始于一次爬巴克斯特山。

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:33.800
<v Father>OK, you guys ready for another Baxter Mountain trip?
<v Father>好的，你们准备好再去一次巴克斯特山了吗？

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:34.560
<v Children>Yeah.
<v Children>是啊。

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:36.230
<v Father>OK.
<v Father>好的。

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:41.200
<v Susan Burton>Every summer, my mom, my dad, Betsy, and I climbed Baxter together. Baxter's a good hike for children.
<v Susan Burton>每年夏天，我妈、我爸、贝齐和我一起爬巴克斯特山。巴克斯特山对于孩子来说是很好的远足地。

00:39:41.200 --> 00:39:43.310
<v Mother>It's 1.1 miles.
<v Mother>它是 1.1 英里。

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:59.180
<v Susan Burton>In this video, Betsy's seven years old. She's adorable. She skips up the trail. She searches the trees for the soft, white semicircles of fungus we break off and take home as souvenirs of the hike. She throws up her tiny arms and beams at my father with giant, brown eyes. She pipes up with little observations about nature.
<v Susan Burton>在这段视频中，贝齐七岁。她很可爱。她在小径上蹦蹦跳跳。她寻找树上那些我们折下来带回家作为远足纪念品的柔软、白色的半圆形真菌。她举起瘦小的双臂，睁着棕色的大眼睛对着我父亲笑。她会观察自然并发表小小的评论。

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:04.910
<v Betsy Burton>Oh, these are roots. They go down into the ground. Let me see if I can find the taproot.
<v Betsy Burton>噢，这些是根。它们深入地下。让我看看能不能找到主根。

00:40:04.910 --> 00:40:06.650
<v Father>What is it called, Betsy?
<v Father>那叫什么，贝齐？

00:40:06.650 --> 00:40:07.270
<v Betsy Burton>The taproot.
<v Betsy Burton>主根。

00:40:07.270 --> 00:40:07.860
<v Father>Taproot?
<v Father>主根？

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:08.880
<v Betsy Burton>Mm-hmm.
<v Betsy Burton>嗯哼。

00:40:08.880 --> 00:40:43.560
<v Susan Burton>When the camera pans to me, it's a different story. Allow me to describe myself. I'm 11, almost 12. I'm wearing a rubber snake around my neck. My t-shirt has a row of teddy bears across the front. Each bear holds a balloon with a letter on it, and together they spell the word "stickers." It is difficult to see my eyes, which are masked by enormous round glasses. I look like an owl, like something from science. I look like what I was at the time, which is a nerd. I regale my family with nuggets from my repository of random nerd facts. When my sister wonders if there are killer bees on Baxter Mountain, I'm ready with the answer.
<v Susan Burton>当摄像机扫向我时，故事就不同了。允许我描述一下我自己。我 11 岁，快 12 岁了。我脖子上戴着一条橡胶蛇。我的 T 恤前面有一排泰迪熊。每只熊拿着一个带有字母的气球，加在一起拼出“贴纸”这个词。很难看到我的眼睛，它们被巨大的圆眼镜遮住了。我看起来像只猫头鹰，像科学产物。我看起来像我当时的样子，即书呆子。我用我的随机书呆子事实库给家人讲段子。当妹妹想知道巴克斯特山上是否有杀人蜂时，我已经准备好了答案。

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:44.470
<v Betsy Burton>Are there killer bees around here?
<v Betsy Burton>这儿附近有杀人蜂吗？

00:40:44.470 --> 00:41:11.330
<v Susan Burton>Not last summer, but the summer before, I checked out a book at the library. And it was all about these killer bees who were killing people. And then when I got back home to Grand Rapids, I read a story in the paper, and it said that there's killer bees in South America, and they should be coming up here in about five years or something. And then it was 1983. So they should be coming up about 1988.
<v Susan Burton>去年夏天没有，但前年夏天，我从图书馆借了一本书。全是关于这些杀死人的杀人蜂的。然后当我回到大急流城，我在报纸上读到一个故事，说南美有杀人蜂，它们大约五年后会来到这里。当时是 1983 年。所以它们应该会在 1988 年左右来到。

00:41:11.330 --> 00:41:34.240
<v Ira Glass>Not long after the hike up Baxter, I started seventh grade at a new school in my Michigan hometown, Central Middle School. I'd gone to my old school since I was three and loved it, and now that I was at Central Middle, I missed it like crazy. Longings for it would well up inside me every Thursday night, when the "Sometimes you want to go where everybody knows your name" song played before Cheers. But it wasn't long before everybody knew my name at Central Middle.
<v Ira Glass>爬完巴克斯特山不久后，我在密歇根老家的一所新学校——中央初中开始了七年级。我从三岁起就一直在我的旧学校，很爱它，现在既然在中央初中，我疯狂地想念它。每到周四晚上，《干杯》剧集前播放那首“有时你想去每个人都知道你名字的地方”的歌时，思念之情就会在我心中汹涌。但没过多久，每个人都知道我在中央初中的名字了。

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:59.580
<v Ira Glass>Around Halloween, we received our first report cards in homeroom. My homeroom teacher was Mr. Bixler. He taught earth science and had a fondness for puns. He liked to add fake names to the attendance slip, like Eileen Dover or Justin Case. Mr. Bixler shuffled through the pile of report cards on his desk. Suddenly he held one up to the light, as if verifying its authenticity. "Susie Burton," he said. "Five A's and two A pluses."
<v Ira Glass>万圣节前后，我们在班主任课上收到了第一张成绩单。我的班主任是比克斯勒先生。他教地球科学，偏爱双关语。他喜欢在点名册上加假名字，比如艾琳·多佛或贾斯汀·凯斯。比克斯勒先生翻着桌上的一叠成绩单。突然他把一张举到光线下，好像在验证真实性。“苏西·伯顿，”他说，“五个 A，两个 A 加。”

00:41:59.580 --> 00:42:19.790
<v Ira Glass>The boy in our homeroom with Tourette's Syndrome let out a series of profanities and yelps. I wanted to flee, but my desk was surrounded. Kids were actually coming over to see my report card. "She even got an A in gym," one boy exclaimed.
<v Ira Glass>我们班上有图雷特综合征的男孩发出了一连串亵渎和尖叫。我想逃跑，但我的课桌被围住了。孩子们真的走过来想看我的成绩单。“她甚至在体育课都得了 A，”一个男孩惊呼道。

00:42:19.790 --> 00:42:51.530
<v Ira Glass>I'd gotten off on the wrong foot at Central Middle. I had blind enthusiasm for spelling bees, book bowls, and science olympiads. I was the only girl in architectural drafting. My other elective was called brain teasers. I did the wrong things, and I looked the wrong way too. I had a short curly perm that hadn't come out the way it was supposed to, braces on my top and bottom teeth, and weird clothes like culottes. Everyone else at my school wore jeans and Reeboks. I wasn't allowed to wear either.
<v Ira Glass>我在中央初中没开好头。我对比拼拼、书球和科学奥林匹克竞赛有着盲目的热情。我是建筑制图课上唯一的女孩。我的另一门选修课叫脑筋急转弯。我做了错误的事情，看起来也错了。我留着一个没烫好的卷发，上下牙带着牙套，穿着奇怪的衣服，比如裙裤。学校里其他人穿牛仔裤和锐步鞋。我不被允许穿这两样。

00:42:51.530 --> 00:43:16.810
<v Ira Glass>I'd had one pair of jeans in my life. I was permitted to get them for a school play. It was a one-act about a Michael Jackson concert, authored by a sixth grader at my old school. At the store, my mother pulled out dark blue Lee jeans with pinstripes. She said that my father might like this pair. They resembled business suit pants.
<v Ira Glass>我这辈子只有一条牛仔裤。我是因为学校演出才被允许买的。那是一部关于迈克尔·杰克逊演唱会的一幕剧，由我旧学校的六年级学生创作。在商店里，我母亲拿出了带有细条纹的深蓝色李维斯牛仔裤。她说我父亲可能喜欢这一条。它们看起来像商务西裤。

00:43:16.810 --> 00:44:00.550
<v Ira Glass>My parents didn't understand. My father said that the boys in architectural drafting teased me because they liked me, all 27 of them. My mother said middle school didn't matter, that college was where you met your real friends. I needed to improve. I needed a manual. So I got a subscription to Seventeen magazine. Seventeen gave advice on a lot of things, like fresh face makeup that would let me shine through, and what I should do if I was better looking than my best friend. But before I could apply any of it at Central Middle, my parents got divorced. My grandfather said it was because we all read at the dinner table. I disagreed, but it didn't matter. We were selling our house, and my mom and Betsy and I were moving to Boulder, Colorado.
<v Ira Glass>我父母不理解。我父亲说建筑制图课上的男孩嘲笑我，是因为他们喜欢我，那是全部 27 个男孩。我母亲说初中不重要，大学才是你结交真正朋友的地方。我需要改进。我需要一本手册。所以我订阅了《十七》杂志。十七杂志给出了很多事情的建议，比如能让我容光焕发的清新妆容，还有如果我比最好的朋友好看我该怎么办。但在我能在中央初中运用这些建议之前，我父母离婚了。外公说这是因为我们都在餐桌上读书。我不同意，但这不重要。我们要卖房子，我妈、贝齐和我搬到了科罗拉多州的博尔德。

00:44:00.550 --> 00:44:31.600
<v Ira Glass>My mother told me that she liked the idea of the west, of the frontier, of going there to make a fresh start. I liked the idea too. In the west, I would be popular. I would reinvent myself. Nobody would know that back in Michigan, I'd been a nerd. And best yet, I would attend Fairview High School, where a junior named Holly had just won the Seventeen cover model contest. I had the issue upstairs in my room, carefully filed in a little library of teendom.
<v Ira Glass>母亲告诉我她喜欢西部的概念，边疆的概念，去那里重新开始。我也喜欢这个主意。在西部，我会很受欢迎。我会重塑自己。没人会知道我在密歇根曾经是个书呆子。最棒的是，我会去费尔维尤高中，那里的一名大三学生霍莉刚赢得了十七封面模特大赛。那期的杂志在我楼上房间里，小心地存放在我的少女小图书馆里。

00:44:31.600 --> 00:45:18.470
<v Ira Glass>By now, I'd been a student of popularity for two years. I did this with the intensity and seriousness with which I'd learned the migratory patterns of the killer bee. I had cassettes full of songs I'd taped off the radio. I'd accumulated the right clothes. I applied the scientific method to wardrobe selection. I typed up a list of my outfits on the Macintosh and posted it inside my closet. The list only applied to weekdays, and I did not deviate from the order. There were 16 outfits, so that there would always be an irregular beat built in, so that I wouldn't always wear Benetton rugby shirt on Mondays or the Guess overalls on Tuesday. The amazing thing is, the plan worked. We moved. I was popular. It was the fresh start any nerd dreams of. It was better than any article that had ever been in Seventeen.
<v Ira Glass>那时候，我已经研究受欢迎度两年了。我带着学习杀人蜂迁徙模式的强度和严肃性来做这件事。我有存满从电台录下的歌曲的磁带。我攒够了合适的衣服。我将科学方法应用于衣橱选择。我在麦金塔电脑上打出我的服装清单，贴在衣柜里。该清单仅适用于工作日，而且我不偏离顺序。共有 16 套服装，所以总会有不规则的节奏，这样我就不会总是在周一穿贝纳通橄榄球衫，或者周二穿 Guess 背带裤。神奇的是，计划奏效了。我们搬家了。我很受欢迎。这是每个书呆子梦想的重新开始。它比十七杂志刊登过的任何文章都要棒。

00:45:18.470 --> 00:46:04.490
<v Ira Glass>I had a group of friends. We all sat together every day at lunch on our jean jackets in a circle in the front courtyard. We French braided each other's hair. We traded clothes. We had slumber parties. On weekdays, we'd often ride bikes until just before dark. One time, we were riding to the grocery store to get ingredients for chocolate chip cookies, and my shoe fell off in the crosswalk. It became a famous story. There was a famous story about me. The littlest things, things that probably didn't matter to anyone else, made me so happy. I saved any note anyone ever wrote to me in class. There was a girl in the house behind me named Shannon, and one night, we yelled to each other from our windows before we turned off our lights and went to bed. It had only happened that once, but I thought about it a lot. It was like something from a movie, like a dream life. And there's video documentation of this too.
<v Ira Glass>我有一群朋友。我们每天午餐都坐在前院的牛仔夹克上围成一圈。我们给彼此编法式辫子。我们交换衣服。我们举办过夜派对。在工作日，我们经常骑自行车直到天黑前。有一次，我们正骑车去杂货店买做巧克力曲奇的配料，我的鞋在人行横道上掉了下来。这成了一个著名的故事。有一个关于我的著名故事。琐碎的事情，对别人可能无关紧要的事情，让我如此快乐。我保存了任何人在课上给我写的任何纸条。我家后面那栋房子的女孩叫香农，一天晚上，我们在关灯上床睡觉前从窗口互相大喊。那只发生过那一次，但我经常想它。就像电影里的东西，就像梦想生活。而且也有这段视频记录。

00:46:04.490 --> 00:46:06.680
<v Susan Burton>Susie? Hi. What should we do?
<v Susan Burton>苏西？嗨。我们该干什么？

00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:09.260
<v Ira Glass>This is me on the telephone in Colorado.
<v Ira Glass>这是在科罗拉多州电话里的我。

00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:26.100
<v Susan Burton>Well, I don't know. I heard a lot of people were going there, but remember what happened last night? OK, well, should we do that? Well, I don't know. OK, it's only, like, 7:30. So why don't we wait until, like, 8:00? And then we'll figure out something, OK? OK, should I call Lisa?
<v Susan Burton>好吧，我不知道。我听说很多人都要去那里，但记得昨晚发生了什么吗？好吧，我们要那样做吗？好吧，我不知道。好吧，才 7:30 左右。所以为什么我们不等到 8:00 左右呢？然后我们会想出点什么的，好吗？好吧，我该打给丽莎吗？

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:34.760
<v Ira Glass>In this video, I have long, straight, shiny blond hair. I have lip gloss, contact lenses, and perfect braceless teeth. I talk on the phone so much my mother has subscribed to call waiting.
<v Ira Glass>在这段视频中，我留着长长、笔直、闪亮的金发。我有唇彩、隐形眼镜和完美的没牙套的牙齿。我打电话太多，母亲给我办了呼叫等待。

00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:55.930
<v Susan Burton>And then-- hold on a sec. Hello? Hello? Oh, gosh, my call waiting signal just messed up. OK, but anyway, you can come over, and we'll braid my hair. OK? And then we'll go down to Pearl Street, OK? OK, bye.
<v Susan Burton>然后——等一下。喂？喂？噢，天哪，我的呼叫等待信号搞砸了。好吧，不管怎样，你可以过来，我们编编我的头发。好吗？然后我们去珍珠街，好吗？好吗，再见。

00:46:55.930 --> 00:47:16.120
<v Ira Glass>The rules of being popular seemed easy to me. Anybody could have done it. The main ones were, smile a lot. Wear good clothes. Giggle. Be a little ditzy. I started remembering to forget my lunch. But the main number one rule of being popular was, don't let anyone know you're smart. On US Government quizzes, I answered questions wrong on purpose.
<v Ira Glass>受欢迎的规则对我来说似乎很简单。任何人都做得到。主要的几条是，多微笑。穿好看的衣服。咯咯笑。稍微傻点。我开始记得忘记带午餐。但受欢迎的头号规则是，不要让任何人知道你很聪明。在政府知识测验中，我故意答错问题。

00:47:16.120 --> 00:47:52.470
<v Ira Glass>But the notes we passed in language arts provided me with a real opportunity to shine. If my friend Joanna wrote, "Antigone sucks, and it's so cold. I wish we didn't have to walk home today," I would write back, "I know. I should of worn a sweater today." But I would spell "should've" like "should" and then the word "of," instead of the contraction for "should have." I'd continue, "Ms. C is totally on the rag today. She's bothering me alot." That's "alot," spelled as one word, no space.
<v Ira Glass>但我们在语言艺术课上传的纸条给我提供了真正大放异彩的机会。如果我的朋友乔安娜写道，“安提戈涅烂透了，太冷了。我希望我们今天不用走回家，”我会回复，“我知道。我今天应该穿件毛衣的。”但我会把“should've”拼成“should”和“of”这两个词，而不是“should have”的缩写。我会继续，“C老师今天在闹情绪。她很烦我。”那是“alot”，拼成一个词，没空格。

00:47:52.470 --> 00:48:20.910
<v Ira Glass>Things were happening so naturally that I could almost convince myself that my life had always been this way. One Saturday night, a group of us went to see the movie, Can't Buy Me Love. The movie's about a nerd who decides that his senior year of high school, he's going to become popular at last. He pays the most beautiful girl in school to go out with him. It didn't even cross my mind to identify with the nerd. I was in so deep that I identified with the pretty blonde cheerleader.
<v Ira Glass>事情发展得如此自然，以至于我几乎可以说服自己我的生活一直都是这样。一个周六晚上，我们一群人去看了电影《不能买爱》。电影是关于一个书呆子决定他在高三时要变得受欢迎。他付钱给学校最美丽的女孩带他出去。我甚至没想过把自己和书呆子对应起来。我陷得太深，以至于我认同了那个漂亮的金发啦啦队长。

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:30.650
<v Susan Burton>Like sands through the hourglass, so are the days of our lives. I'm Susan Burton and
<v Susan Burton>像沙漏里的沙子一样，这就是我们生活的日子。我是苏珊·伯顿，

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:32.310
<v Betsy Burton>Betsy Burton.
<v Betsy Burton>还有贝齐·伯顿。

00:48:32.310 --> 00:48:33.830
<v Susan Burton>And these are
<v Susan Burton>而这些是

00:48:33.830 --> 00:48:36.210
<v Betsy Burton>The days of our lives.
<v Betsy Burton>我们生活的日子。

00:48:36.210 --> 00:48:45.980
<v Susan Burton>These last two weeks, watching video of myself from those days, I saw something that wasn't clear to me at the time. Here's a tape Betsy and I made for our dad for Father's Day, two years after we moved to Boulder.
<v Susan Burton>过去这两周，看着那个时代的我的视频，我看到了当时不清楚的事情。这是贝齐和我在我们搬到博尔德两年后，为父亲节制作的录像带。

00:48:45.980 --> 00:48:56.170
<v Susan Burton>We just wanted to make this video for you for Father's Day because we didn't want to get you a book, because we always do that. And this way you can look at us whenever you want to.
<v Susan Burton>我们只是想为你父亲节制作这段视频，因为我们不想给你买书，因为我们总是那样。这样你任何时候想我们的时候都能看。

00:48:56.170 --> 00:49:04.820
<v Ira Glass>I look exactly right, studiously plain hair, silver bracelets, pretty clothes. But I'm still a nerd. Here I am, explaining the music of the young people to my dad.
<v Ira Glass>我看起来完全正确，精心打扮的头发，银色手镯，漂亮的衣服。但我依然是个书呆子。这就是我，向爸爸解释年轻人的音乐。

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:08.360
<v Young Susan Burton>And then, oh, Depeche Mode. Here's some Depeche Mode.
<v Young Susan Burton>然后，噢，追随者乐队。这儿是一些追随者乐队。

00:49:08.360 --> 00:49:10.910
<v Susan Burton>I can't resist translating the name of the band.
<v Susan Burton>我无法抗拒翻译乐队的名字。

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:21.690
<v Young Susan Burton>Depeche means "hurried." Mode means "fashion." Hurried fashion. Or dispatch fashion. Yeah, it's dispatch. Because it's not sous depeche.
<v Young Susan Burton>Depeche 的意思是“匆忙”。Mode 的意思是“时尚”。匆忙时尚。或者调度时尚。是的，是调度。因为不是 sous depeche。

00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:49.810
<v Susan Burton>In this video, what I see now is how hard I'm trying. I've blowdried my hair and matched my socks to my shorts, even though it's a summer morning and we're just staying home. I'm sarcastic in a way I know is meant to mimic two of my friends from school. I make a show of talking about driver's permits, even though I was terrified of the day I'd eventually get behind the wheel. The popular girl is in this video. There's proof that she exists. But it's clear that the killer bee girl is right inside her.
<v Susan Burton>在这段视频中，我现在看到的是我努力了多少。我吹干了头发，把袜子和短裤搭好，即使那是个夏天的早晨，我们只是待在家里。我以一种我知道是为了模仿两个朋友的方式讽刺。我做出一副谈论驾驶执照的样子，即使我对以后坐在方向盘后面那一天的到来感到恐惧。那个受欢迎的女孩在这段视频里。有证据表明她存在。但显然，那个杀人蜂女孩就藏在她里面。

00:49:49.810 --> 00:49:57.240
<v Young Susan Burton>OK, I think that'll end my instructional video. As you can see, [UNINTELLIGIBLE] are better.
<v Young Susan Burton>好的，我想我的教学视频到此结束。正如你所看到的，[听不清] 更好。

00:49:57.240 --> 00:49:59.690
<v Susan Burton>I didn't change that much.
<v Susan Burton>我没改变那么多。

00:49:59.690 --> 00:50:01.720
<v Ira Glass>Susan Burton is a writer in New York City.
<v Ira Glass>苏珊·伯顿是纽约市的作家。

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:24.240
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "NEW LIFE" BY DEPECHE MODE]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 追随者乐队的《新生活》]

00:50:24.240 --> 00:50:32.630
<v Ira Glass>Act Five, Just Shoot Me. We end our program today with this story from David Sedaris, a story asking the simple question, why even bother to make home movies?
<v Ira Glass>第五幕，尽管拍吧。我们今天以大卫·塞达里斯的这个故事结束节目，这个故事问了一个简单的问题：为什么还要费心制作家庭录像呢？

00:50:32.630 --> 00:51:00.190
<v David Sedaris>It was the day before Thanksgiving, and my mother and I were at the Raleigh airport waiting for my sister Gretchen to return from Rhode Island. You could still smoke then, and so we sat beside the ashtray, passing the time as the plane rolled up to the gate. A young woman took a seat not far away, and when she pulled a camcorder from her knapsack, my mother groaned and prodded me with her elbow. "Oh, God, here we go."
<v David Sedaris>那是感恩节的前一天，我和母亲在罗利机场等着我妹妹格雷琴从罗德岛回来。那时还可以抽烟，所以我们坐在烟灰缸旁边，打发时间，看着飞机滑向登机口。一个年轻女人坐在不远处，当她从背包里掏出一台摄像机时，母亲呻吟着，用胳膊肘顶了顶我。“噢，上帝，又来了。”

00:51:00.190 --> 00:51:33.470
<v David Sedaris>The camera was new and sleek, and as she aimed it towards a doorway, my mother covered the side of her face. "They shouldn't allow people to walk around with things like that," she said. "I mean, really. There should be a law." Had the young woman been carrying a loaded rifle, my mother might have brushed it off. But she hated having her picture taken. "Whoever she's waiting for might have it coming, but what about us poor nobodies caught in the crossfire? I don't want to be in some stranger's piss-poor movie," she said. "I don't even want to be in my own."
<v David Sedaris>摄像机崭新且精致，当她把它对准门口时，母亲遮住了半张脸。“他们不该让人拿着那种东西到处走，”她说。“我是说，真的。应该有条法律。”如果那个年轻女人拿着一把上膛的步枪，母亲可能还会不以为意。但她讨厌被人拍照。“不管她在等谁，那是她的事，但我们这些无辜的过路人被困在交火中又算什么？我不想出现在某个陌生人的烂电影里，”她说。“我甚至不想出现在我自己的电影里。”

00:51:33.470 --> 00:51:49.320
<v David Sedaris>The young woman overheard and looked our way with an expression reading, "Who would want to videotape you?" I smiled, and she left her seat to stand against a column.
<v David Sedaris>那个年轻女人听到了，向我们这边看了一眼，表情写着，“谁想拍你啊？”我笑了，她离开座位站在柱子旁。

00:51:49.320 --> 00:52:18.760
<v David Sedaris>"Why do people need to get everything on film?" my mother asked. "Are they that afraid they're going to forget something? And why do it here? Can't it at least wait for the parking lot?" The exit door opened, and passengers stepped out into the terminal. The young woman started filming, and my mother scowled as complete strangers waved into the camera. We were well out of range, but still she kept her hand to the side of her face, looking as though she were talking about the person seated next to her.
<v David Sedaris>“为什么人们需要把一切都拍下来？”母亲问道。“他们真的那么害怕会忘记什么吗？而且为什么要在这儿拍？难道不能至少等到停车场吗？”出口打开了，乘客们走进候机楼。年轻女人开始拍摄，母亲皱着眉头，看着完全的陌生人对着摄像机挥手。我们离得够远，但她仍然捂着脸，看起来好像在和旁边座位上的人说话。

00:52:18.760 --> 00:52:38.030
<v David Sedaris>"Now, this is just ridiculous," she said. "I mean, what? Do you honestly think anyone's going to watch a thing like this? What's the point in making a movie when all anyone is doing is stepping off a damn airplane? It's not like it hasn't been done. It's not like there are any surprises here, so why bother with the $800 camera?"
<v David Sedaris>“现在，这太荒谬了，”她说。“我是说，什么？你真的以为有人会看这种东西吗？拍电影的意义是什么，当所有人做的只是走下该死的飞机？又不是没拍过。这里面又没什么惊喜，所以为什么要费心用那个 800 美元的摄像机呢？”

00:52:38.030 --> 00:52:52.220
<v David Sedaris>My sister Gretchen stepped into the terminal and wondered why her mother was covering her face. "Do you have a toothache?" she asked. "Did somebody slap you?"
<v David Sedaris>我妹妹格雷琴走进候机楼，纳闷母亲为什么捂着脸。“你牙疼吗？”她问。“有人打你了吗？”

00:52:52.220 --> 00:53:19.430
<v David Sedaris>There were more video cameras awaiting us at the baggage claim. A grown man filmed a toddler pounding against the side of a suitcase. A teenage boy shot his father hoisting a golf bag. "I've got to get out of here," my mother said. And then she went outside to wait in the car.
<v David Sedaris>行李领取处有更多的摄像机在等着我们。一个成年男人拍着一个幼儿拍打手提箱侧面。一个少年拍摄他父亲搬运高尔夫球包。“我得离开这儿，”母亲说。然后她走到外面在车里等着。

00:53:19.430 --> 00:53:51.960
<v David Sedaris>Once, while at the beach, my father borrowed a movie camera and spent the afternoon following us around. We were young then and excited by the thought of watching ourselves projected onto a bed sheet. He shot us swimming and eating and piling on top of our mother who had tried in vain to escape. "This is going to be good," he said. "Come on, now. Pin her down, so I can get a good shot of her face."
<v David Sedaris>曾经有一次在海滩，父亲借了一台电影摄像机，花了一下午时间跟着我们。那时我们还小，想到能看着自己被投射在床单上感到兴奋。他拍我们游泳、吃饭、扑向试图逃跑却徒劳的母亲。“这会很有趣，”他说。“来吧，现在。按住她，这样我就能拍到她脸部的特写。”

00:53:51.960 --> 00:54:19.190
<v David Sedaris>My sister Lisa would later suggest that there had been no film in the camera, but I don't think our father had it in him to be that cruel. Most likely, he set it aside, meaning to have it developed, but never quite finding the time. Either that or our mother got to it first. She was good at destroying film.
<v David Sedaris>我妹妹丽莎后来暗示摄像机里根本没有胶片，但我认为父亲没那么残忍。很可能，他把它放在一边，打算去冲洗，但一直没腾出时间。要不就是母亲先动了手。她很擅长销毁胶片。

00:54:19.190 --> 00:55:01.300
<v David Sedaris>After she died, we took turns holding her various belongings to our noses. Mom's coat, Mom's washcloth, Mom's pillow. This is what people do when they're left with such paltry iconography. They smell things, continuing long after the scent has faded, and it feels silly to stand in the living room sniffing your mother's hairbrush. She'd been dead for three years when my aunt sent a home movie she'd transferred to videotape. This was film shot in the mid-'70s, when she and my uncle had gone with our parents to the Virgin Islands.
<v David Sedaris>她去世后，我们轮流把她的各种遗物凑近鼻子闻。妈妈的外套，妈妈的洗脸巾，妈妈的枕头。当人们剩下这些寒酸的肖像画时就是这样。他们嗅着东西，一直持续到气味散去很久之后，站在客厅里嗅母亲的发梳确实显得很傻。她去世三年后，阿姨寄来了一盘她转录到录像带上的家庭录像。那是 70 年代中期拍摄的，那时她和叔叔和我父母去了维尔京群岛。

00:55:01.300 --> 00:55:46.100
<v David Sedaris>My sisters and I put the tape into the VCR and watched as my father silently pleaded with a locked door. We witnessed our aunt and uncle strolling white beaches and merrily waving from the deck of a boat. It wasn't until the end that we finally saw our mother and then only briefly. Whoever shot the footage had caught her unaware, walking out the door of a restaurant and slowly moving into the sun. It was such a small thing, our mother snapping shut her purse and putting one foot in front of the other, but it seemed to us like a miracle, like the way the movies must have seemed when they were very first invented.
<v David Sedaris>我和姐妹们把录像带放进录像机，看着父亲默默地向一扇锁着的门祈求。我们目睹了阿姨和叔叔漫步在白色海滩上，在船甲板上欢快地挥手。直到最后，我们才终于看到了母亲，而且只有短暂的一刻。不管是谁拍摄的，拍到了她不知情的样子，正走出餐厅的门，缓慢地步入阳光中。那是多么小的一件事，母亲合上钱包，一步接着一步，但在我们看来就像奇迹，就像电影最初发明时那样。

00:55:46.100 --> 00:56:21.250
<v David Sedaris>On seeing the camera, she covered her eyes and ran back inside. "Get lost," we imagined her saying. But this was the beauty of video. Our mother retreated into the restaurant, and, against her better wishes, we hit the rewind button, drawing her back out. She was ours now. And we sat there for hours, none of us speaking except to say, "Do it again."
<v David Sedaris>看到摄像机时，她捂住眼睛跑回了室内。“走开，”我们想象她说。但这正是视频的美妙之处。母亲退回餐厅，违背她的意愿，我们按下了倒带键，把她拉了出来。她现在属于我们了。我们坐在那儿看了好几个小时，谁也没说话，除了说：“再来一次。”

00:56:21.250 --> 00:56:26.330
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris is the author of several books. His most recent is When You Are Engulfed in Flames.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯是几本书的作者。他最近的作品是《当你被火焰吞噬时》。

